00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  wednesday july 13th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  for the last couple of years spacex has  been launching satellites into orbit to  form starlink an internet providing web  that as of june was serving 400 000  customers globally  but spacex says its signals could be  hampered if the federal communications  commission approves new rules to expand  the spectrum for 5g wireless service  it's facing opposition though  tv operator dish  wants those frequencies added to support  its future business plans  so how is the battle shaking out and  what will it mean for internet customers  joining us to discuss this is wsj  telecoms reporter drew fitzgerald  hey drew thanks for coming on the show  sure thanks for having me  let's just start with some basics what  exactly is the fcc doing and what would  it mean in terms of you know how we use  the internet and get access to it what  the fcc is reviewing is whether it can  change its rules for wireless spectrum  and these are the radio waves over the  air that cell phones satellite tvs  satellite internet services airplanes  that are connected to wi-fi it's what  they all use to communicate with the  wider world a particular frequency band  that is mostly used for satellite  service right now  that's what this is all about some  companies want the rules rewritten to  make room for 5g service from cell phone  towers  while others want to keep it restricted  to satellite use as it is today so the  debate is really all about internet  service and specifically the best way to  provide that service over the year  so dish network is one of the companies  that's in favor of this change how would  it benefit them why would they support  it well dish is actually a tv company  that's been slammed by cord cutting like  everybody else so it's chairman has made  a big bet on 5g networks that are built  on the ground  and dish has another big supporter in a  company owned by michael dell another  billionaire who's invested in telecom  and a lot of technology obviously before  okay but you know companies like spacex  they don't want regulators to do this  what's their argument  well spacex's internet business which is  called starlink has been putting  thousands of satellites into orbit over  the past few years and those satellites  use some of these frequencies today to  communicate with their customers  spacex's main argument against the rule  changes is that 5g service in this  particular band of frequencies it's  saying that that's going to interfere or  drown out the signal from its existing  satellites  so pretty much spacex is arguing if you  change the rules that its internet isn't  going to be as robust as it is at the  moment  exactly it's arguing that if the rules  change in the way that some are  proposing  that  its service will be degraded and  the internet speeds and the reliability  that they have achieved so far from  satellites in the sky  will be lessened and that a lot of their  customers won't have internet service  that's as good as it is today  and a lot of starlink's customers are in  remote areas where there's not cable  internet or any other kind of high-speed  internet service available so that  leaves their customers with few choices  if in fact their service is going to be  affected  so dish and its supporters are in favor  of these changes spacex and other  companies like it don't want the changes  to come through  how are these companies stepping up  their efforts to convince regulators to  take their side  well they're stepping up their lobbying  game both sides they've been going to  the federal communications commission  many times over the past few months  making their case in meetings and  in filings  arguing that the science is basically on  their side  ultimately what this is is an  engineering decision and it's up to the  fcc  to decide not what the new rules should  be but basically whether the rules  should be examined whether they should  be changed at all and whether it's  possible for these two types of internet  services these two types of technologies  to co-exist in the same airwaves in the  same airspace  so that's what's before the fcc i should  also add that both companies have spent  a decent amount of time through their  lobbyists on capitol hill  and there have been hearings held on  this and  also a lot of activity on capitol hill  from both sides trying to marshal  senators and members of congress to make  the case for them in washington i mean  this seems like a really technical  argument from both sides but it is  essentially about internet use so should  regular internet customers be concerned  about how this debate shakes out  well starlink argues that they should  and that's another development that's  happened over the past few weeks  from spacex the company sent emails to a  lot of its customers essentially urging  them to write their congress person and  to write the fcc  urging these officials to not change the  status quo and to protect their internet  service and that's something that a lot  of companies will commonly do they'll  marshal a lot of their customers to make  their case in washington  we'll see how much of an effect that has  like many of elon musk's companies the  starlink customer base is a very  passionate customer base and after just  a few weeks there have been more than 95  000 comments filed in the system  on this particular issue but at the end  of the day it's not the politicians but  it's civil servants career  engineering types that are looking at  this record and deciding what to do next  do we have any timeline on when we  should expect a decision from them  it's really up to the fcc there's no  stop clock on this and  it could go on for weeks it could go on  for years  all right we'll just have to keep an eye  on it and see how things shake out  that's our reporter drew fitzgerald  thanks for joining us drew thanks for  having me  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  and if you like our show please rate and  review it you can do that wherever you  get your podcasts  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  you

